  ### Step 4: The Title Clue — Uncovering the Slack Space

  If you try to decrypt out7.enc with the old keys from #avidreader13.log, OpenSSL outputs bad decrypt.

  Where are the real keys?

  • Look at the challenge name: UnforgottenBits.
  • In digital forensics, "unforgotten bits" refers to Slack Space (the unused bytes at the end of a disk block).
  • Check the notes folder. File notes/1.txt contains only 12 bytes (chizazerite\n), but the filesystem allocated a full 4096-byte block (block 56274).

  Extract the raw block:

    blkcat -o 731136 disk.flag.img 56274 | xxd

  Behind the word chizazerite, you find a 1,905-character binary string with decimal points:

    01010010100.01001001000100.01001010000100.00101010010101...
  ──────
  ### Step 5: Decode the Golden Ratio Base (φ-base)

  How do you decode this binary string?
  Check .lynx/browsing-history.log:

    https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Church_encoding
    https://cs.lmu.edu/~ray/notes/numenc/
    https://www.wikiwand.com/en/Golden_ratio_base

  Yone was reading about Golden Ratio Base (Phinary):

  • Uses the golden ratio: φ = (1 + √5)/2 ≈ 1.6180339887.
  • Notice that the string has 127 decimal points.
  • Total length = 1,905 characters ⟹ 1905/127 = exactly 15 characters per token (11 integer bits + . + 3 fractional bits).
  • 127 characters is the exact length of an ASCII string containing:
  salt=<16 hex> key=<64 hex> iv=<32 hex>.

  Run this Python script to decode the slack space:

    import math, subprocess
    
    phi = (1 + math.sqrt(5)) / 2
    
    # Read block 56274 slack space
    raw = subprocess.check_output(['blkcat', '-o', '731136', 'disk.flag.img', '56274'])
    slack = raw[12:].decode('latin1', errors='ignore').strip('\x00').strip()
    
    # Split into 15-character tokens
    tokens = [slack[i*15 : (i+1)*15] for i in range(len(slack) // 15)]
    
    def decode_token(token):
        int_p, frac_p = token.split('.')
        # Integer part: powers of phi >= 0 (from right to left)
        val = sum(phi**i for i, b in enumerate(reversed(int_p)) if b == '1')
        # Fractional part: negative powers of phi (from left to right)
        val += sum(phi**(-(j+1)) for j, b in enumerate(frac_p) if b == '1')
        return chr(round(val))
    
    decoded_text = "".join(decode_token(t) for t in tokens)
    print(decoded_text)

